Why This Is Asked
Time skew in multi-region systems is a common but often overlooked root cause of incorrect analytics and deduplication failures. This question tests the ability to define concrete metrics, set safety thresholds, and implement practical containment and rollback strategies without requiring complex redesign.

Key Concepts
- Event-time vs. processing-time semantics
- Clock synchronization (NTP, monotonic clocks)
- Deduplication keys and idempotent processing
- Dead letter queues and reorder buffers
- Safe rollback criteria and validation

Code Example
```javascript
// Idempotent event processor with deduplication
async function processEvent(event) {
  const cacheKey = `event:${event.event_id}`;

  if (await redis.setnx(cacheKey, 'processing')) {
    try {
      await redis.expire(cacheKey, 86400); // 24-hour TTL
      await processInOrder(event);
    } finally {
      await redis.del(cacheKey);
    }
  } else {
    await sendToDLQ(event, 'duplicate');
  }
}

// Monotonic timestamp enforcement
function enforceMonotonic(event, sourceState) {
  if (event.timestamp >= sourceState.lastTimestamp) {
    sourceState.lastTimestamp = event.timestamp;
    return event;
  }
  return null; // Reject out-of-order event
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design robust global traffic shaping, fault isolation, and controlled rollback mechanisms under partial-region degradation.

## Key Concepts
- Per-region circuit breakers and quotas
- Regional backpressure and buffering
- Canary testing and safe rollback
- Metrics: p95 latency, error rate, saturation, queue depth

## Code Example
```javascript
// Sketch of per-region breaker state
class RegionalBreaker {
  constructor(threshold, windowMs = 60000) {
    this.threshold = threshold;
    this.window = windowMs;
    this.failures = 0;
    this.successes = 0;
    this.state = 'CLOSED';
    this.lastFailureTime = 0;
  }

  recordSuccess() {
    this.successes++;
    if (this.state === 'HALF_OPEN' && this.successes > this.threshold) {
      this.state = 'CLOSED';
      this.failures = 0;
      this.successes = 0;
    }
  }

  recordFailure() {
    this.failures++;
    this.lastFailureTime = Date.now();
    const errorRate = this.failures / (this.failures + this.successes);
    if (errorRate >= this.threshold) {
      this.state = 'OPEN';
    }
  }

  canAttemptRequest() {
    if (this.state === 'OPEN') {
      const timeSinceFailure = Date.now() - this.lastFailureTime;
      if (timeSinceFailure > this.window) {
        this.state = 'HALF_OPEN';
        return true;
      }
      return false;
    }
    return true;
  }
}

// Token bucket quota per region
class RegionalQuota {
  constructor(maxTokens, refillRate) {
    this.tokens = maxTokens;
    this.maxTokens = maxTokens;
    this.refillRate = refillRate;
    this.lastRefill = Date.now();
  }

  tryConsume(tokens = 1) {
    this.refill();
    if (this.tokens >= tokens) {
      this.tokens -= tokens;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }

  refill() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const tokensToAdd = (now - this.lastRefill) * this.refillRate / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.maxTokens, this.tokens + tokensToAdd);
    this.lastRefill = now;
  }
}
```

This implementation provides:
- **Circuit Breaker**: Tracks failure rates and transitions between CLOSED, OPEN, and HALF_OPEN states
- **Token Bucket Quota**: Enforces rate limits per region with configurable refill rates
- **Observable Metrics**: Both classes support instrumentation for monitoring p95 latency, error rates, and quota utilization

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design per-tenant budgets, dynamic throttling, and robust rollback strategies in a multi-tenant, latency-sensitive service.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant SLOs and budgets
- Burn rate calculation and budget ledger
- Edge throttling (token bucket) and graceful degradation
- Canary rollouts and automated rollback
- Observability with per-tenant metrics and PromQL

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: update per-tenant budget on each request
function onRequest(req){
  const tenant = req.headers['tenant-id'];
  const now = Date.now();
  const budget = ledger.getBudget(tenant);
  if (budget.remaining <= 0) throttle(req, tenant);
  else { forward(req); ledger.consume(tenant, estimateCost(req)); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this system under simulated traffic bursts?
- What thresholds would you choose for alerting and auto-rollback cadence?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical incident engineering: how to fuse disparate signals, assign accountability, and make safe change decisions under pressure.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-signal correlation across metrics/logs/traces
- SLO-aligned alert routing and ownership mapping
- Canary and synthetic testing for regression prevention
- RCA templating and rollback playbooks

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple rule mapping alerts to on-call owners
const owners = {
  payments: "team-payments",
  auth: "team-auth",
  inventory: "team-inventory"
};
function route(alert) {
  const svc = alert.tags?.service || "general";
  return owners[svc] || "oncall";
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the end-to-end runbook in a staging environment?
- What metrics would you collect to validate the success of the rollback strategy?
